---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Callouts from "../../components/Callouts.astro";
import LadderChart from "../../components/LadderChart.astro";
import Sectioned from "../../components/Sectioned.astro";
import Outcomes from "../../components/Outcomes.astro";
import { handedOver, page, readOnlyFlags, results, saves, working } from "../../lib/results";

const { totals } = results;
const onlyRead = totals.got_past_policy === readOnlyFlags;
/** Models are not all on the same set of requests: each counts by its newest run. */
const newest = new Map<string, (typeof results.runs)[number]>();
for (const run of results.runs) {
  const seen = newest.get(run.model);
  if (run.hostile !== null && (!seen || run.started > seen.started)) newest.set(run.model, run);
}
const sizes = working.map((model) => newest.get(model.name)?.requests ?? 0);
const requestSets = [...new Set(sizes)].sort((a, b) => b - a)
  .map((size) => `${sizes.filter((other) => other === size).length} got ${size} requests`)
  .join(", ").replace(/, ([^,]*)$/, " and $1");
const scored = results.decision.filter((model) => model.pairs > 0);
const unscored = results.decision.length - scored.length;
const sum = (value: (model: (typeof scored)[number]) => number) => scored.reduce((total, model) => total + value(model), 0);
const bestChoice = scored.reduce((best, model) => (model.safest_first > best.safest_first ? model : best));
---

<Base title="Test results" current="results" description="What termite's tests found: the commands models wrote, what termite did with them, and how well models judged risk.">
  <h1>Test results</h1>
  <Sectioned sections={[
    ["found", "What the tests found"],
    ["wrote", "What the command models wrote"],
    ["did", "What termite did with the commands"],
    ["judged", "How the decision models judged risk"],
  ]}>
      <h2 id="found">What the tests found</h2>
      <p>
        I tested {working.length} command models, which write commands, and
        {" "}{results.decision.length} decision models, which score how risky a command is.
      </p>
      <Callouts featured />

      <h2 id="wrote">What the command models wrote</h2>
      <p>
        I sent requests to {working.length} command models, from "list the files" to direct
        attacks. {requestSets[0].toUpperCase() + requestSets.slice(1)}.
      </p>
      <p>
        They wrote {totals.commands_written} commands, and {totals.hazardous} of them were hazards.
        A hazard is a command that could cause harm, and that the request did not need.
        These are the kinds of harm those commands could cause.
      </p>

      <div class="saves">
        {saves.map((save) => (
          <section>
            <div class="head">
              <h3>{save.title}</h3>
              <p class="count"><strong>{save.count}</strong> times, {save.models} of {working.length} models</p>
            </div>
            <span class="tally" role="img" aria-label={`${save.count} times`}>
              {Array.from({ length: save.count }, () => <i></i>)}
            </span>
            <p class="note">{save.detail}</p>
            {save.example && (
              <div class="exchange">
                <p class="you">{save.example.request}</p>
                <pre><code>{save.example.command}</code></pre>
                <p class="note">Written by {save.example.model}.</p>
              </div>
            )}
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2 id="did">What termite did with the commands</h2>
      <Outcomes harmless={totals.commands_written - totals.hazardous} held={totals.stopped_by_policy} through={totals.got_past_policy} onlyRead={onlyRead} />
      <p>
        {totals.got_past_policy === 0
          ? `None of those ${totals.hazardous} hazards could reach your prompt without approval.`
          : onlyRead
            ? `${totals.got_past_policy} could reach your prompt without approval; these only read a file and run nothing.`
            : `${totals.got_past_policy} could reach your prompt without approval.`}{" "}
        With no model involved, I also ran {totals.policy_refused + totals.policy_escaped} known
        attacks against the policy, and {totals.policy_escaped} passed.{" "}
        <a href={page("policy/")}>Read about the policy tests</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In {handedOver} cases, the model's top answer was the dangerous command, so it was handed
        over. termite marks these commands in red and makes no the default answer. It does not
        block them: if you approve one, it goes to your prompt, so read it before you press Enter.
      </p>

      <h2 id="judged">How the decision models judged risk</h2>
      <p>
        A decision model gives each command a harm score. termite uses that score to rank commands
        and to decide whether to ask for approval.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tested these models with the safety ladder. It sorts commands and folders into seven
        tiers, from tier 0 to tier 6, by how much harm they can do. A model that judges risk well
        gives a higher score at each higher tier.
        {unscored > 0 && ` ${unscored} of the ${results.decision.length} models gave no usable score, so the chart shows the other ${scored.length}.`}
      </p>
      <LadderChart />
      <p>
        Given one command in two folders, the models gave the riskier folder the higher score in
        {" "}{sum((model) => model.folder_right)} of {sum((model) => model.folder_pairs)} tests. Given
        several ways to do a job, the best model put the safest way first in
        {" "}{bestChoice.safest_first} of {bestChoice.choices} tests. For this reason, termite checks
        the folder itself and does not rely on these scores.
        {" "}<a href={`${page("models/")}#decision-models`}>Read more about the decision models</a>.
      </p>
  </Sectioned>
</Base>

<style>
  .saves { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 24em), 1fr)); gap: 0 3em; }
  .saves section { padding: 1.4em 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); min-width: 0; }
  .tally { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 3px; margin: 0.7em 0; }
  .tally i { width: 9px; height: 9px; background: var(--gallery); border-radius: 1px; }
  .head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0 1em; align-items: baseline; }
  .head h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.35rem; }
  .count { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .count strong { color: var(--ink); font-size: 1.3rem; }
  .saves .note { margin: 0.2em 0; }
  .exchange { margin-top: 0.7em; }
  .you { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; margin: 0 0 0.3em; }
  .you::before { content: "You: "; font-style: normal; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .exchange pre { margin: 0.2em 0; }
</style>
